WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.300
A cormorant at sea has salt water all around.

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.460
Its kidneys cannot make urine as salty as the sea, so where does its extra salt go?

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.620
The first film balanced a fish's water and salt in a ledger of gains and losses.

4
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.080
A sea fish pumps its extra salt out across its gills.

5
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.540
A bird has no gills.

6
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:31.343
Clearing seawater's salt through its kidneys alone would cost the bird more water

7
00:00:31.343 --> 00:00:35.083
than it drank. The ledger cannot balance that way.

8
00:00:36.343 --> 00:00:37.663
Is there another route out?

9
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:47.127
Knut Schmidt-Nielsen, C. B. Jørgensen and H. Osaki tested that question.

10
00:00:48.387 --> 00:00:50.447
They worked with double-crested cormorants.

11
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:55.010
They gave the birds a load of salt, by mouth or by infusion.

12
00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:00.370
Then they collected two separate fluids from each bird, and compared them.

13
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:05.743
One vial caught fluid from the cloaca, which is urine mixed with feces.

14
00:01:07.243 --> 00:01:10.583
The other caught a clear fluid that dripped from the tip of the beak.

15
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:13.373
Before the results, make a guess.

16
00:01:14.693 --> 00:01:16.853
Which vial do you think will be the saltier one?

17
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.510
If the kidney route ran saltier, the cloacal vial would plot higher.

18
00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.530
Instead, the beak vial filled with a concentrated salt solution.

19
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.030
The cloacal fluid stayed much less salty.

20
00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:41.873
On the bar, the beak fluid reached about the level of seawater, or somewhat more.

21
00:01:43.073 --> 00:01:44.473
And the glands made it fast.

22
00:01:45.913 --> 00:01:48.933
Steady secretion could clear the body's salt within hours.

23
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:54.113
The fluid came from paired glands in the head, the nasal salt glands.

24
00:01:55.413 --> 00:02:00.053
It ran out through the internal nostrils inside the mouth, and dripped from the tip

25
00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:01.053
of the beak.

26
00:02:01.617 --> 00:02:02.757
Back to the ledger.

27
00:02:04.037 --> 00:02:05.677
Getting rid of salt costs water.

28
00:02:06.877 --> 00:02:11.277
The saltier the gland fluid, the less water each bit of salt takes with it.

29
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.430
Gulls reach higher salt levels still.

30
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:21.190
In a later experiment on a gull, the salt gland removed many times as much salt as

31
00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:22.190
the cloaca.

32
00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:27.463
The experiment showed a second route for salt, one that bypasses the kidney.

33
00:02:28.763 --> 00:02:33.303
It did not show how the gland concentrates salt, or that every bird has one.

34
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:36.773
The bookkeeping is the same as the fish's.

35
00:02:37.713 --> 00:02:42.693
The bird's second route out is a gland, and how salty it runs sets the water cost.
